Begin Exploring Related Columns
Most relevant tables / columns and how they will be used  

• `Cytogen.RefNo`, `Cytogen.CaseNo` : case identifiers to be joined with CytoConverter output  
• `Cytogen.Morph` : filter for breast‑cancer cases (morphology code = '3111')  
• `Cytogen.Topo`  : filter for adenocarcinoma cases (topography code = '0401')  
• `CytoConverted.RefNo`, `CytoConverted.CaseNo` : case identifiers (join to Cytogen)  
• `CytoConverted.InvNo`, `CytoConverted.Clone` : investigation / clone granularity (not absolutely required for the cohort filter, but useful if we want clone‑level counts)  
• `CytoConverted.Chr`, `CytoConverted.Start`, `CytoConverted.End` : genomic interval to be mapped to bands  
• `CytoConverted.Type` : copy‑number category produced by CytoConverter.  We will inspect the actual value set to find out how “Gain”, “Loss”, “Amp(lification)” and “HomDel” (or similar) are encoded.  
• `CytoBands_hg38.chromosome`, `CytoBands_hg38.cytoband_name`,  
  `CytoBands_hg38.hg38_start`, `CytoBands_hg38.hg38_stop` : genomic bins (bands) that the CytoConverter intervals will be intersected with.  
• `CytoBands_hg38.ChrOrder` (not present but we infer it is the alphabetical chromosome order; if it is missing we can derive it from the cytoband chromosome string) – needed for the requested sorting.  

Core conditions that will be used in the final query  

```
WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')        -- breast cancer or adenocarcinoma
```

and later, when mapping genomic intervals to bands  

```
ON cv.Chr = b.chromosome
AND cv.Start  < b.hg38_stop
AND cv.End    > b.hg38_start          -- interval overlap
```

Below are ten exploratory / build‑up BigQuery SQL queries (each different, limited to 20 rows) that gradually move from simple inspection to the full aggregation needed for the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. Description: list distinct morphology codes present in Cytogen
SELECT DISTINCT Morph
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: list distinct topology codes present in Cytogen
SELECT DISTINCT Topo
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: sample of breast‑cancer (Morph = '3111') cases
SELECT RefNo, CaseNo, Age, Sex, Country
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: sample of adenocarcinoma (Topo = '0401') cases
SELECT RefNo, CaseNo, Morph, Topo
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Topo = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: CytoConverter rows belonging to the breast/adeno cohort (no band mapping yet)
SELECT cv.RefNo, cv.CaseNo, cv.Chr, cv.Start, cv.End, cv.Type
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cv
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`      AS c
  ON c.RefNo  = cv.RefNo
 AND c.CaseNo = cv.CaseNo
WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: what distinct 'Type' values does CytoConverter produce, and how many?
SELECT Type, COUNT(*) AS n_rows
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`
GROUP BY Type
ORDER BY n_rows DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: preliminary counts of each Type in the breast/adeno cohort
SELECT cv.Type, COUNT(*) AS n_rows
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cv
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
  ON c.RefNo  = cv.RefNo
 AND c.CaseNo = cv.CaseNo
WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')
GROUP BY cv.Type
ORDER BY n_rows DESC;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: inspect how one CytoConverter interval overlaps with cytobands
SELECT cv.RefNo, cv.CaseNo, cv.Chr, cv.Start, cv.End,
       b.cytoband_name, b.hg38_start, b.hg38_stop
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cv
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
  ON c.RefNo  = cv.RefNo
 AND c.CaseNo = cv.CaseNo
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` AS b
  ON cv.Chr = b.chromosome
 AND cv.Start < b.hg38_stop
 AND cv.End   > b.hg38_start
WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: per‑band counts of each CNV type (still raw counts, no frequency)
SELECT b.chromosome,
       b.cytoband_name,
       cv.Type,
       COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(cv.RefNo,'_',cv.CaseNo,'_',cv.InvNo,'_',cv.Clone)) AS n_events
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted`   AS cv
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`         AS c
  ON c.RefNo  = cv.RefNo
 AND c.CaseNo = cv.CaseNo
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38`  AS b
  ON cv.Chr = b.chromosome
 AND cv.Start < b.hg38_stop
 AND cv.End   > b.hg38_start
WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')
GROUP BY b.chromosome, b.cytoband_name, cv.Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: final aggregation – number & frequency (%) of each copy‑number class per band,
--                  ordered by chromosome ordinal then band start/stop.
--                  Assumes CytoConverter.Type contains exactly the four needed categories:
--                  'Gain1' (1 extra copy), 'Gain>1' (amplification), 'Loss1' (1 copy lost),
--                  'Loss2' (homozygous deletion).  Adjust the CASE expression below to match
--                  the exact wording revealed by query #6.
WITH cohort AS (
  SELECT cv.RefNo, cv.CaseNo, cv.InvNo, cv.Clone,
         cv.Chr, cv.Start, cv.End,
         CASE
           WHEN LOWER(cv.Type) LIKE '%amp%'                     THEN 'Amplification'
           WHEN LOWER(cv.Type) = 'gain'                         THEN 'Gain'
           WHEN LOWER(cv.Type) = 'loss'                         THEN 'Loss'
           WHEN LOWER(cv.Type) LIKE '%homdel%' OR LOWER(cv.Type) = 'loss2' THEN 'HomDel'
           ELSE 'Other'
         END AS class
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cv
  JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
    ON c.RefNo  = cv.RefNo
   AND c.CaseNo = cv.CaseNo
  WHERE (c.Morph = '3111' OR c.Topo = '0401')
),
band_map AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT
         CONCAT(RefNo,'_',CaseNo,'_',InvNo,'_',Clone) AS event_id,
         class,
         b.chromosome            AS Chr,
         b.cytoband_name         AS Band,
         b.hg38_start            AS BandStart,
         b.hg38_stop             AS BandStop
  FROM cohort AS cv
  JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.CytoBands_hg38` AS b
    ON cv.Chr = b.chromosome
   AND cv.Start < b.hg38_stop
   AND cv.End   > b.hg38_start
),
per_band AS (
  SELECT Chr, Band, BandStart, BandStop,
         SUM(class = 'Amplification') AS n_amp,
         SUM(class = 'Gain')          AS n_gain,
         SUM(class = 'Loss')          AS n_loss,
         SUM(class = 'HomDel')        AS n_homdel,
         COUNT(DISTINCT event_id)     AS total_events
  FROM band_map
  GROUP BY Chr, Band, BandStart, BandStop
),
with_freq AS (
  SELECT *,
         ROUND(100 * n_amp    / total_events, 2) AS freq_amp,
         ROUND(100 * n_gain   / total_events, 2) AS freq_gain,
         ROUND(100 * n_loss   / total_events, 2) AS freq_loss,
         ROUND(100 * n_homdel / total_events, 2) AS freq_homdel
  FROM per_band
),
-- create numeric chromosome order so X=23, Y=24 etc
ordered AS (
  SELECT *,
         CASE
           WHEN Chr = 'chrX' THEN 23
           WHEN Chr = 'chrY' THEN 24
           ELSE CAST(SUBSTR(Chr,4) AS INT64)
         END AS chr_ord
  FROM with_freq
)
SELECT Chr, Band, BandStart, BandStop,
       n_amp,  freq_amp,
       n_gain, freq_gain,
       n_loss, freq_loss,
       n_homdel, freq_homdel
FROM ordered
ORDER BY chr_ord, BandStart, BandStop
LIMIT 20;
```

The last query (#10) delivers, for every cytogenetic band, the number of events and their frequency (%) for the four requested copy‑number classes, and sorts the result by chromosome ordinal followed by the band genomic start/stop coordinate.  Adjust the `CASE` mapping in `cohort` if query #6 shows slightly different wording for CytoConverter’s copy‑number types.
